re  targeted a conversation on what what  should be done and some of that is  better done behind closed doors then buy  a diploma megaphone diplomacy meantime  you know we have the situation on the  ground on the dynamic dramatically  shifting we have Russia's Syria Turkey  really now the key players after US  troops pulled out of northern Syria and  I'm curious to hear how you see the  shifting dynamic and potentially a  changing role for Europe I think really  what has happening has a first and  foremost strengths into the role of the  Assad regime it has strengthened the  role of Russia in Syria the potential  for the EU to contribute to a solution  so far is rather limited I think if the  chance is there to extend the ceasefire  further and give roof was for a  political agreement political solution  on what should be done this would  require at some point not only an  agreement with Russia I would probably  also mandate from the UN Security  Council the you should be willing to  make a constructive contribution to  create a buffer zone that would stop  military violence for more sustained  period but I think that the stakes on  this are high it would require a protic  greement with Syrian government with  Russia and will probably at some point  also require the willingness of EU  countries to commit peace troops to this  region which I think would be a major  challenge but that's wishful thinking  isn't it I mean what if the ceasefire is  not extended because Turkey has only  agreed to a pause in fighting for five  days how concerned are you about what  will happen to the Kurds in the area  when it runs out on Tuesday I think  that's that's something where the top  priority needs to be to extend that  ceasefire I think we should keep all  options open on increasing pressure on  Turkey but on the other end I think any  solution were required to also take into  account a Turkey's legitimate interest  and to do everything we can to maintain  turkeys alliance with 